  "query_preview": "..\nMonday through Thursday: An upper trough swinging through the \nregion overnight will exit into Montana this morning. As this \ntrough exits a dry northwest flow pattern develops over the region\nfollowed by high pressure gradually building into Tuesday and \nWednesday and lingering into Thursday. This will allow for a \nwarming trend with low relative humidity each day. Given the \nnorth- northwest flow today more rounds of smoke will impact the \nregion from regional wildfires, including smoke coming down from \nBritish Columbia. Then with lighter winds and building high \npressure the smoke issues will likely continue. Several air \nquality agencies have issued Air Quality Alerts for several \ncounties across Central and Eastern Washington and north Idaho.\nCurrent temperature forecasts are from the National Blend of\nModels. Depending on amount of smoke cover, temperatures could not\nreach potential and end up cooler than forecast.\n\nFriday through Sunday: Ensemble means show high pressure \nbeginning to flatten late in the week, but with dry air \npersisting. The zonal flow and dry air mass will bring an uptick \nin wind speeds with ECMWF ensembles showing 15-25 MPH gusts across\nEastern Washington each day, and 25-35 MPH through the Cascade \ngaps. This has the potential to bring elevated to locally \ncritical fire weather conditions. JW",
